 ## Subtopics
 
 ### Particle Emission Update
-Updates the voxel texture for particle systems (e.g., fire) by adding emission color without averaging. It calculates emission based on a power function of an emission map and opacity, then adds this to the previous voxel value.
-- **When to choose**: Use for dynamic particle effects like fire or smoke where emission is additive and non-averaging.
-- **Link**: [Particle Emission Update](1001-particle-emission-update.md) (hypothetical link, as no article exists).
+Updates the voxel texture for particle systems (e.g., fire) by adding emission color without averaging [S2, unofficial]. It calculates emission as $\text{emission} = \text{pow}(u\_emission, \text{vec3}(gammaEditor))$, opacity as $\text{opacity} = \text{texture}(u\_emissionMap, b\_uv).r \times u\_opacity$, and color as $\text{color} = \text{vec4}(\text{emission} \times \text{opacity}, 0.0)$ [S2, unofficial]. It loads the previous voxel value, adds the new color, and stores the result [S2, unofficial]. When to choose: Use for dynamic particle effects like fire or smoke where emission is additive and non-averaging [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity**: Linear with respect to the number of surface voxels $N$ for conversion and upload [S1, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity**: $O(N)$ for the voxel array and $O(W \times H)$ for the texture [S1, unofficial].
-- **Strengths**: Efficient transfer of surface data to GPU; uses standard OpenGL texture formats [S1, unofficial].
-- **Limitations**: Limited by `GL_MAX_TEXTURE_SIZE`; packing 6 components into 4 components is ambiguous [S1, unofficial].
-- **When to use**: When surface voxel data needs to be rendered on the GPU using ray-marching [S1, unofficial].
